{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon Bedrock expands support for Service Quotas - AWS","context":"Amazon Bedrock, a managed service for enterprise-grade access to high-performing foundation models from leading AI companies, now supports viewing inference quotas for the bedrock-mantle endpoint through AWS Service Quotas. This allows customers to track limits for the endpoint in a familiar way and gain visibility into the limits that apply to their workloads. The bedrock-mantle endpoint supports OpenAI Responses API, OpenAI Chat Completions API, and Anthropic Messages API, enabling customers to run existing OpenAI or Anthropic based applications on Amazon Bedrock with minimal code changes. AWS Service Quotas now exposes per-model input-tokens-per-minute and output-tokens-per-minute quotas for supported models on the endpoint. Customers can view their current quotas by opening the AWS Service Quotas console, choosing Amazon Bedrock, and searching for \"Bedrock Mantle\". To request an increase to any quotas, follow the standard Amazon Bedrock limit increase process. Service Quotas support for the bedrock-mantle endpoint is available in all AWS Regions where the endpoint is offered, including US East (N. Virginia, Ohio), US West (Oregon), Asia Pacific (Mumbai, Tokyo, Sydney, Jakarta), Europe (Frankfurt, Ireland, London, Milan, Stockholm), and South America (São Paulo)","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/5/amazon-bedrock-service-quotas/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon Bedrock launches a redesigned console optimized for OpenAI- and Anthropic-compatible APIs - AWS","context":"Amazon Bedrock, a managed service offering secure access to high-performing foundation models from leading AI companies, has launched a redesigned console optimized for OpenAI- and Anthropic-compatible APIs. The new console experience is designed to facilitate experimentation, iteration, and scaling. It supports the OpenAI Responses API, OpenAI Chat Completions API, and Anthropic Messages API through the bedrock-mantle endpoint. The console allows users to browse the full Amazon Bedrock model catalog, compare models side by side based on capabilities, modality support, context window, and applicable service quotas, and organize work into projects for streamlined evaluation and usage insights. Each project includes project-aware documentation with automatically prefilled model ID, region, endpoint URL, and API key reference for easy integration into applications. The new console is available in all AWS Regions where the bedrock-mantle endpoint is offered. To get started, users can sign in to the AWS Management Console, open Amazon Bedrock, and choose the new experience. They can then create a project, pick a model, and send requests through the bedrock-mantle endpoint using existing OpenAI or Anthropic client libraries and an Amazon Bedrock API key","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/06/amazon-bedrock-redesigned-console-optimized-openai-anthropic-compatible-apis/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon Bedrock announces OpenAI-compatible Projects API - AWS","context":"Amazon Bedrock, a fully managed AI service, has introduced OpenAI-compatible Projects API support in its Mantle inference engine. This update enables users with multiple applications, environments, or teams to create individual projects for better isolation. Each project can have distinct IAM-based access control and tags for enhanced cost visibility. The Projects API is accessible to all customers using OpenAI-compatible APIs, including the Responses API and Chat Completions API, through Mantle inference engine in Amazon Bedrock. There is no extra charge for utilizing the Projects API; users only pay for the underlying model inference consumption. To initiate using the Projects API in Amazon Bedrock, refer to the Amazon Bedrock documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/03/amazon-bedrock-projects-api-mantle-inference-engine/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"amazon-bedrock","skill_description":"Builds generative AI applications on Amazon Bedrock. Covers model invocation (Converse API, InvokeModel), RAG with Knowledge Bases, Bedrock Agents, Guardrails, and AgentCore (including the Harness managed agent loop). Applies when invoking models, setting up or migrating Knowledge Bases to Managed, deploying to AgentCore, migrating/porting/converting a Bedrock Agent (including inline agents) to an AgentCore Harness, troubleshooting Bedrock errors (ThrottlingException, AccessDeniedException), or choosing Bedrock models (Claude, Llama, Nova, Titan). Also for prompt caching, quota and throttling diagnosis, cost tracking, migrating between Claude model generations, chunking strategies, and Bedrock model selection. Covers AgentCore Payments (x402, microtransactions, Payment Manager, Connector, Instrument, Coinbase CDP, Stripe Privy, paid endpoints, agent payments). NOT for custom model training, Rekognition, or Comprehend. For SageMaker model discovery, customization, or endpoints, use aws-ai-ml.\n\nServices: bedrock, bedrock-runtime, bedrock-mantle, bedrock-agent, bedrock-agent-runtime, bedrock-agentcore-control, bedrock-agentcore\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize, design, migrate\nPersona: developer, devops, architect\nWorkload: generative-ai, rag, agents","skill_name":"amazon-bedrock"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-ai-ml","skill_description":"Selects, deploys, and customizes AI models on Amazon SageMaker. Training or Processing jobs, fine-tuning (SFT/DPO/RLVR/RLAIF), model selection, evaluation, SageMaker or Bedrock deployment, inference optimization and endpoint diagnostics. Use when fine-tuning models on SageMaker, choosing a base model to customize or deploy from SageMaker JumpStart or Hub, SageMakerPublicHub, or the model catalog, checking data quality (duplicates, languages, PII), transforming or validating data (reformat, clean, filter, dedupe, split, merge, redact), generating synthetic data, labeling data with a model, human review or annotation and applying its corrections, evaluating model quality, deploying or optimizing endpoints, configuring IAM/S3, or managing SageMaker Managed MLflow. Use for endpoint health, latency, logs, metrics, errors. Covers Serverless Model Customization, Nova and OSS deployment paths, and PySDK v3. NOT for SageMaker Ground Truth labeling jobs, Feature Store, or general AWS infrastructure.\n\nServices: sagemaker, s3, iam, bedrock\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, data-scientist\nWorkload: ai-ml","skill_name":"aws-ai-ml"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Search AWS docs for bedrock-mantle endpoint details"}
